# Java Coding Standards for Minecraft Fabric Mod

## General Standards
- Use Java 21 features and syntax
- Follow Minecraft/Fabric naming conventions
- Use proper package structure: `com.example.modid` or `org.yourorg.mods.modname`
- Implement `ModInitializer` interface for main mod class

## Fabric-Specific Guidelines
- Use `net.fabricmc.api.ModInitializer` for mod entry point
- Use `org.slf4j.Logger` for logging (not `System.out.println`)
- Use `net.minecraft.util.Identifier` for resource locations
- Use `net.minecraft.recipe.Ingredient` for recipe ingredients
- Register items, blocks, and entities properly with registries

## Code Organization
- Keep mod logic simple and focused
- Use JSON recipes when possible instead of custom Java classes
- Log initialization and important events
- Follow Minecraft's existing code patterns
- Separate client and server code appropriately
- Use interfaces for API exposure

## Package Structure
```
com.example.mymod/
├── MyMod.java              // Main mod class
├── init/                   // Initialization classes
│   ├── ModItems.java
│   ├── ModBlocks.java
│   └── ModRecipes.java
├── items/                  // Custom item classes
├── blocks/                 // Custom block classes
├── client/                 // Client-only code
└── util/                   // Utility classes
```

## Example Structure
```java
package com.example.mymod;

import net.fabricmc.api.ModInitializer;
import org.slf4j.Logger;
import org.slf4j.LoggerFactory;

public class MyMod implements ModInitializer {
    public static final String MOD_ID = "mymod";
    public static final Logger LOGGER = LoggerFactory.getLogger(MOD_ID);

    @Override
    public void onInitialize() {
        LOGGER.info("Initializing {} mod", MOD_ID);
        
        // Initialize items, blocks, etc.
        ModItems.register();
        ModBlocks.register();
        ModRecipes.register();
    }
}
```

## Best Practices
- Use constants for mod ID and other repeated strings
- Handle null checks and edge cases
- Document public APIs with Javadoc
- Use meaningful variable and method names
- Follow single responsibility principle
- Minimize mixin usage when possible

## Language-Specific Clarifications

### TypeScript Rules Do Not Apply
**IMPORTANT**: User rules mention TypeScript standards (no `any`, no `!`, no `as unknown as T`), but these are **TypeScript-specific** and do NOT apply to Java code:
- Java has its own type system (primitives, objects, generics)
- Java uses different type safety mechanisms
- Follow Java conventions, not TypeScript conventions

### String Formatting for Java
User rules specify double quotes and string templates for TypeScript/JavaScript. **For Java code**, use standard Java conventions:
- **String literals**: Use double quotes (`"`) - this is standard Java
- **String concatenation**: Using `+` operator is acceptable and common in Java
- **String formatting**: Use `String.format()`, `MessageFormat`, or `StringBuilder` for complex formatting
- **String templates** (Java 21+): Optional feature, can be used but not required
- **Performance**: Use `StringBuilder` for building strings in loops or performance-critical code

Example:
```java
// Standard Java string concatenation (acceptable)
String message = "Hello " + name + "!";

// String formatting (preferred for complex formatting)
String message = String.format("Hello %s!", name);

// StringBuilder for performance-critical code
StringBuilder sb = new StringBuilder();
sb.append("Hello ").append(name).append("!");
String message = sb.toString();
```

## Basic Storage Mod Specific Standards

### Code Style
- Use proper indentation (tabs as shown in existing code)
- Follow existing naming conventions
- Add meaningful comments for complex logic
- Use proper Java logging with LOGGER.info() or LOGGER.debug()
- Package: `com.khazoda.basicstorage.*`

### Minecraft Fabric Specific
- Main class: `BasicStorage` implements `ModInitializer`
- Client class: `BasicStorageClient` for client-side initialization
- Use `Identifier.of("basicstorage", "name")` for resource identifiers
- Register blocks/items using Fabric registries: `Registry.register(Registries.BLOCK, ...)`
- Use data components for item storage
- Constants defined in `Constants.java` class

### Storage System Patterns
```java
// Constants definition
public static final String BS_NAMESPACE = "basicstorage";
public static final String BS_NAME = "Basic Storage";
public static final Logger BS_LOG = LoggerFactory.getLogger(BS_NAME);

// Registry registration
Registry.register(Registries.BLOCK, Identifier.of(Constants.BS_NAMESPACE, "name"), block);
```

### Registry Pattern
```java
// Block registration
Registry.register(Registries.BLOCK, Identifier.of(Constants.BS_NAMESPACE, "name"), block);
Registry.register(Registries.ITEM, Identifier.of(Constants.BS_NAMESPACE, "name"), new BlockItem(block, new Item.Settings()));

// Item registration
Registry.register(Registries.ITEM, Identifier.of(Constants.BS_NAMESPACE, "name"), item);
```

### Data Component Pattern
- Use data components for item storage
- Register components in `DataComponentRegistry`
- Access via `ItemStack.get(ComponentType)`

### Error Handling
- Always check if blocks/items exist before accessing
- Use proper exception handling for mod initialization
- Log errors appropriately with LOGGER.error()
- Handle missing components gracefully

### Testing
- Test crate creation and storage
- Verify item storage and retrieval works correctly
- Test crate station functionality
- Verify data component persistence
- Test both client and server functionality
